! conclusion: Use the daily scan as a time-bounded depositing heartbeat whose external response tests the consulting-time hypothesis.

  + context: War Room decision WR-6 (2026-04-13). Operator: Joe. The test window is the twenty days of M-daily-scan.

  + IF:
    The stack needs its first structured depositing activity and a test of the twenty-hours-per-month consulting hypothesis.

  + HOWEVER:
    The hypothesis has no supporting external response merely because scanning activity occurs.

  + THEN:
    Run the twenty-day M-daily-scan test and use at least one consulting conversation by day twenty as its success condition. If none occurs, revise the approach. Monitor the test through the War Machine's frame-derivative pipeline.

  + BECAUSE:
    A countable external conversation distinguishes a depositing heartbeat from activity that never reaches another party.

    + evidence: War Bulletin 7 defines the twenty-day M-daily-scan as the first structured depositing activity, sets one consulting conversation by day twenty as the evidential threshold, and assigns monitoring to the frame-derivative pipeline.
